---
import LegalPage from '../../components/LegalPage.astro';
import { COMPANY } from '../../lib/company';

const mail = `mailto:${COMPANY.email}`;
---

<LegalPage title="Privacy policy" description={`How ${COMPANY.registeredName} uses your personal information.`}>
  <p>
    This explains what personal information we collect when you use this website or buy from us, why, and your
    rights. We only collect what we need, and we never sell your information.
  </p>

  <h2>Who's responsible for your information</h2>
  <p>
    {COMPANY.registeredName} (company number {COMPANY.registeredNumber}, registered office {COMPANY.registeredOffice})
    is the controller of your personal information. Contact us about anything in this policy at{" "}
    <a href={mail}>{COMPANY.email}</a>.
  </p>

  <h2>What we collect and why</h2>
  <p>
    Data protection law only lets us use your information if we have a good reason, and it gives those reasons
    official names (called a "legal basis"). For each item below we say, in plain English, what the reason is.
  </p>

  <h3>When you buy something</h3>
  <ul>
    <li>
      <strong>Your name and email address</strong> — to process your order, send your receipt, put you on the door list
      for a show, and contact you about your order.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Your delivery address and phone number</strong> (merch only) — to deliver your order and contact you about
      delivery.
    </li>
    <li><strong>What you bought, when, and how much you paid</strong> — to fulfil your order and keep our accounts.</li>
    <li>
      <strong>Payment details</strong> — your card details are collected and processed by Stripe, our payment provider.
      We never see or store your full card number.
    </li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    <strong>Why we're allowed to:</strong> when you buy something you're making an agreement with us, and we can't
    deliver your order or let you into a show without these details. The law calls this{" "}
    <strong>"performing a contract"</strong>. We also have to keep records of sales for tax and company accounts, so
    we keep order and payment details for as long as the law says — this is called a <strong>"legal obligation"</strong>.
  </p>

  <h3>When you email us</h3>
  <p>We use your email address and what you write to reply to you. That's all.</p>
  <p>
    <strong>Why we're allowed to:</strong> if your email is about an order, it's part of our agreement with you
    (<strong>"performing a contract"</strong>, as above). Otherwise, it's simply that you've contacted us and
    replying is a reasonable thing to do — the law calls this a <strong>"legitimate interest"</strong>: a genuine
    reason that you'd expect, and that doesn't override your rights.
  </p>

  <h3>Keeping the site and payments secure</h3>
  <p>
    To stop bots, fraud and stolen cards, our website host (Cloudflare) looks at basic technical details such as your
    IP address (your device's internet address) and which browser you're using, and Stripe checks each payment for
    signs of fraud. These checks are automatic. Very occasionally they may block a genuine payment by mistake — if that
    happens to you, email us and a real person will look into it.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>Why we're allowed to:</strong> keeping the site, your payment and our business safe from fraud is a{" "}
    <strong>"legitimate interest"</strong> (explained above). We only use these details for security, never for
    advertising.
  </p>

  <h3>Marketing</h3>
  <p>We don't send you marketing emails unless you've separately and explicitly agreed to receive them.</p>

  <h2>Who we share it with</h2>
  <p>We use trusted service providers who process information on our behalf, under contracts that protect it:</p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Stripe</strong> — payment processing, fraud prevention and receipts.</li>
    <li>
      <strong>Cloudflare</strong> — website hosting, security, bot protection (Cloudflare Turnstile) and forwarding
      emails you send us.
    </li>
    <li><strong>Resend</strong> — sends our order confirmation emails.</li>
    <li><strong>Sanity</strong> — stores our website content and order records.</li>
    <li><strong>Google</strong> — our email provider, if you email us.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    We may also share information if the law requires it (for example with HMRC), or with delivery companies to get
    your order to you.
  </p>

  <h2>International transfers</h2>
  <p>
    Some of these providers store or process information outside the UK, including in the United States. Where they
    do, the transfer is protected by safeguards recognised under UK data protection law, such as the UK–US data
    bridge or the UK's International Data Transfer Agreement / Addendum.
  </p>

  <h2>How long we keep it</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>
      <strong>Order and payment records:</strong> 6 years from the end of the financial year they relate to, as required
      for tax and accounting.
    </li>
    <li><strong>Emails with us:</strong> as long as needed to deal with your query, then deleted.</li>
    <li><strong>Security logs:</strong> kept only briefly by our providers.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2 id="cookies">Cookies</h2>
  <p>
    We don't use advertising or analytics cookies. The only cookies or similar storage set while you shop are
    strictly necessary ones from Stripe (to take payment securely) and Cloudflare (to protect the site from bots).
  </p>
  <p>
    Some content comes from other services, which may set their own cookies under their own privacy policies:
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li>
      <strong>SoundCloud player</strong> — doesn't load until you press "Load SoundCloud player". If you do, your
      browser remembers that choice (a small setting saved in your browser — it's never sent to us) so the player loads
      straight away next time. Clear your browser's site data to undo it.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>YouTube and Vimeo videos</strong> — only load when you press play (or briefly when you hover over one
      with a mouse, to show a preview). We use YouTube's privacy-enhanced mode and Vimeo's "do not track" setting to
      keep their cookies to a minimum.
    </li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Your rights</h2>
  <p>Under UK data protection law you have the right to:</p>
  <ul>
    <li>ask for a copy of the information we hold about you;</li>
    <li>ask us to correct information that's wrong;</li>
    <li>ask us to delete your information (unless we have to keep it by law);</li>
    <li>object to, or ask us to restrict, how we use it;</li>
    <li>ask for your information in a portable format.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    To use any of these rights, email <a href={mail}>{COMPANY.email}</a>. We'll respond within one month. If you're
    unhappy with how we've handled your information, you can complain to the Information Commissioner's Office
    (ICO) at <a href="https://ico.org.uk/make-a-complaint/" rel="noopener noreferrer" target="_blank">ico.org.uk</a>{" "}
    — though we'd appreciate the chance to sort it out first.
  </p>

  <h2>Changes</h2>
  <p>We'll update this page if how we use your information changes. The date at the top shows the latest version.</p>

  <p>See also our <a href="/legal/terms">terms of sale</a>.</p>
</LegalPage>
